1
00:00:01,110 --> 00:00:03,790
Hello everyone, welcome
to our seventh lecture

2
00:00:03,790 --> 00:00:07,630
on respiratory physiology
and as a neuroscientist

3
00:00:07,630 --> 00:00:10,170
this topic is very dear
to my heart because

4
00:00:10,170 --> 00:00:13,830
it is on control
mechanisms of respiration.

5
00:00:13,830 --> 00:00:15,490
I'm Dr. Jörg Litt Hest.

6
00:00:17,710 --> 00:00:19,850
These session objectives are

7
00:00:19,850 --> 00:00:21,890
consistent with the
event description

8
00:00:22,290 --> 00:00:25,330
which is to focus on the
brainstem centers that

9
00:00:25,330 --> 00:00:27,950
control respiration,
central and peripheral

10
00:00:27,950 --> 00:00:31,110
sensors they utilize
and the mechanisms that

11
00:00:31,110 --> 00:00:35,010
operate the effectors. To
integrate this knowledge,

12
00:00:35,010 --> 00:00:37,630
the session features
specific situations in

13
00:00:37,630 --> 00:00:40,970
which which respiratory
functions have to adapt

14
00:00:40,970 --> 00:00:44,670
to higher oxygen demand
or lower oxygen supply.

15
00:00:47,590 --> 00:00:49,610
This is the basic feedback

16
00:00:49,610 --> 00:00:52,170
loop of respiratory control.

17
00:00:52,360 --> 00:00:55,570
Let's begin with the
sensors in the lower left.

18
00:00:55,650 --> 00:00:58,530
There are various kinds
of sensors that the

19
00:00:58,530 --> 00:01:01,670
system employs. They're
either chemoreceptors

20
00:01:01,670 --> 00:01:05,630
that react to chemicals or
they're mechanoreceptors

21
00:01:05,630 --> 00:01:08,610
that react to
stretch, for instance.

22
00:01:08,990 --> 00:01:12,710
The peripheral
chemoreceptors and those

23
00:01:12,710 --> 00:01:15,210
for the respiratory
system are found in

24
00:01:15,210 --> 00:01:20,190
the carotid bodies or
at the aortic arch.

25
00:01:20,370 --> 00:01:23,470
And then there are central
chemoreceptors which

26
00:01:23,470 --> 00:01:25,770
are found inside the
central nervous system.

27
00:01:26,950 --> 00:01:30,430
The pulmonary
mechanoreceptors are all

28
00:01:30,430 --> 00:01:33,850
associated with structures
of the lung itself

29
00:01:33,850 --> 00:01:40,070
and are located within
the thoracic cavity.

30
00:01:40,410 --> 00:01:44,390
All of these sensors
send information to the

31
00:01:44,390 --> 00:01:48,490
respiratory control center,
which is located in the

32
00:01:48,490 --> 00:01:52,090
medulla and overlaps into
the pons of the brainstem.

33
00:01:52,650 --> 00:01:55,150
The respiratory control center

34
00:01:55,150 --> 00:01:57,170
then sends out information

35
00:01:57,770 --> 00:02:03,110
through the spinal
cord to the effectors.

36
00:02:03,110 --> 00:02:06,370
The effectors are the
respiratory muscles,

37
00:02:06,620 --> 00:02:10,730
and the largest of them is
of course the diaphragm.

38
00:02:11,480 --> 00:02:14,070
This slide adds a
little more detail.

39
00:02:14,720 --> 00:02:17,470
We can begin again
with the receptors,

40
00:02:17,890 --> 00:02:21,570
so that's the aphoran
portion, so that's the portion

41
00:02:21,570 --> 00:02:25,850
that brings information
into the central nervous

42
00:02:25,850 --> 00:02:31,110
system. We have peripheral
chemoreceptors which are

43
00:02:31,110 --> 00:02:35,230
located at the aortic arch
or at the carotid bodies.

44
00:02:35,230 --> 00:02:39,590
They can sense oxygen,
carbon dioxide, and also pH.

45
00:02:39,770 --> 00:02:44,090
This information is
related or relayed to

46
00:02:44,090 --> 00:02:47,510
the brainstem via
cranial nerve 9 and 10.

47
00:02:48,050 --> 00:02:50,590
There are central
chemoreceptors that

48
00:02:50,590 --> 00:02:53,990
are inside the
brain which react to

49
00:02:53,990 --> 00:02:57,470
pH, changes in pH
or hydrogen ions.

50
00:02:58,320 --> 00:03:01,290
There are lung stretch
receptors, those are

51
00:03:01,290 --> 00:03:04,330
mechanoreceptors that
information is relayed

52
00:03:04,330 --> 00:03:07,070
to the brainstem via
cranial nerve 10.

53
00:03:08,510 --> 00:03:12,530
And lastly, there are
muscle and joint receptors

54
00:03:12,530 --> 00:03:17,370
which relay information
to the brainstem as well.

55
00:03:18,530 --> 00:03:23,690
The key portion of the
medullary respiratory

56
00:03:23,690 --> 00:03:27,130
center is the dorsal
respiratory group.

57
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:31,170
We're going to look at
this more in the coming

58
00:03:31,170 --> 00:03:35,490
slides. This group is
influenced and functioned by

59
00:03:35,490 --> 00:03:40,030
an apneustic center, which
is located in the pons.

60
00:03:40,550 --> 00:03:44,090
And this type of
regulation is positive,

61
00:03:44,090 --> 00:03:47,930
meaning that the
apneustic center initiates

62
00:03:47,930 --> 00:03:51,870
a function in the dorsal
respiratory group.

63
00:03:52,030 --> 00:03:55,690
In the pons, there's also
the pneumotoxic center,

64
00:03:55,690 --> 00:04:00,190
and the pneumotoxic
center overall has an

65
00:04:00,190 --> 00:04:04,710
inhibitory effect on the
dorsal respiratory group.

66
00:04:05,270 --> 00:04:08,730
So, depending on all of this
interaction, Information

67
00:04:08,790 --> 00:04:12,330
is then sent out to the
effectors, which, for instance,

68
00:04:12,330 --> 00:04:17,670
is the diaphragm, but also
other respiratory muscles,

69
00:04:17,670 --> 00:04:21,130
and this information travels
via the phrenic nerve.

70
00:04:23,430 --> 00:04:25,850
This image depicts
the brainstem

71
00:04:25,850 --> 00:04:28,530
centers that control breathing.

72
00:04:29,070 --> 00:04:33,090
The most important of them
is the medullary respiratory

73
00:04:33,090 --> 00:04:37,710
center that encompasses
various clusters of nuclei.

74
00:04:37,710 --> 00:04:40,330
as you can see
here. It encompasses

75
00:04:40,330 --> 00:04:42,170
the dorsal respiratory group,

76
00:04:42,290 --> 00:04:45,690
it encompasses the pre
-Britzinger complex,

77
00:04:45,690 --> 00:04:48,510
and also the ventral
respiratory group.

78
00:04:49,030 --> 00:04:51,750
The dorsal respiratory group,

79
00:04:53,750 --> 00:04:58,690
abbreviated DRG
in this lecture,

80
00:04:58,970 --> 00:05:02,080
is involved in the
generation of inspiration

81
00:05:02,080 --> 00:05:05,710
and also in the setting
of the respiratory rate.

82
00:05:06,910 --> 00:05:12,970
This DRG works closely
together with the pneumotaxic

83
00:05:12,970 --> 00:05:18,030
center, which is this
center up here in the pons,

84
00:05:18,210 --> 00:05:21,650
which sends inhibitory signals,

85
00:05:21,950 --> 00:05:24,850
but is also involved
in the regulation of

86
00:05:24,850 --> 00:05:27,950
respiratory rate. So those
two, they work together.

87
00:05:29,830 --> 00:05:34,270
The other respiratory
group that we have

88
00:05:34,270 --> 00:05:35,990
here in the
medullary respiratory

89
00:05:35,990 --> 00:05:39,110
center is the ventral
respiratory group.

90
00:05:39,630 --> 00:05:42,650
And this respiratory
group is involved

91
00:05:42,650 --> 00:05:45,630
in inspiration and
also in expiration,

92
00:05:45,810 --> 00:05:51,230
but has its main function
in the expiratory sector.

93
00:05:52,350 --> 00:06:00,290
This group is inactive
during quiet breathing.

94
00:06:00,290 --> 00:06:04,350
So when only tidal
breathing occurs, there is

95
00:06:04,350 --> 00:06:07,010
no firing from the
ventral respiratory group.

96
00:06:07,230 --> 00:06:12,090
So forced breathing
events are different. So

97
00:06:12,090 --> 00:06:16,210
that is when the ventral
respiratory group engages.

98
00:06:16,210 --> 00:06:19,510
So forced inspiration
or forced expiration.

99
00:06:20,830 --> 00:06:24,950
The pre-Bertzinger
complex is also involved

100
00:06:24,950 --> 00:06:28,110
in rhythm generation,
so it also works

101
00:06:28,110 --> 00:06:31,440
together with the dorsal
respiratory group by

102
00:06:31,830 --> 00:06:36,110
inhibiting dorsal
respiratory group neurons.

103
00:06:37,620 --> 00:06:41,950
Lastly, we have the
apneustic center, and

104
00:06:41,950 --> 00:06:46,910
the apneustic center
sends positive signals to

105
00:06:46,910 --> 00:06:50,090
the dorsal respiratory
group. and by doing

106
00:06:50,090 --> 00:06:54,370
that it controls
breathing intensity so for

107
00:06:54,370 --> 00:06:57,870
instance it controls
the depth of breathing

108
00:07:00,730 --> 00:07:06,070
now all of these centers
they operate in reaction

109
00:07:06,070 --> 00:07:11,330
to central chemoreceptors
so central chemoreceptors

110
00:07:11,330 --> 00:07:13,830
are involved in minute
-to-minute breathing.

111
00:07:14,090 --> 00:07:20,790
So that's the basic function
of the respiratory system,

112
00:07:20,790 --> 00:07:24,510
the basic tidal function
of the respiratory system.

113
00:07:24,750 --> 00:07:30,730
These sensors are nearby
the dorsal respiratory

114
00:07:30,730 --> 00:07:33,330
group. The dorsal respiratory
group would just be

115
00:07:33,330 --> 00:07:36,210
on the other side here. So
this, of course, depicts

116
00:07:36,210 --> 00:07:39,930
the medulla and the
sensors in an area of the

117
00:07:39,930 --> 00:07:43,330
medulla that's called the
ventrolateral medulla.

118
00:07:43,330 --> 00:07:48,010
And there's a rostral,
almost forward cluster,

119
00:07:48,010 --> 00:07:52,690
an intermediate one, and
a caudate receptor area.

120
00:07:54,630 --> 00:07:57,830
The most important
of the chemoreceptors

121
00:07:57,830 --> 00:08:00,230
are the central chemoreceptors,

122
00:08:00,560 --> 00:08:04,810
and they can sense the
pH of the cerebrospinal

123
00:08:04,810 --> 00:08:09,370
fluid directly and carbon
dioxide indirectly.

124
00:08:09,610 --> 00:08:12,890
And I will take you
through now how this works.

125
00:08:13,970 --> 00:08:17,750
So for instance, if
there's a buildup of

126
00:08:17,750 --> 00:08:21,730
CO2 in capillary blood
going to the brain,

127
00:08:21,730 --> 00:08:25,490
then the CO2, because
it's lipophilic,

128
00:08:25,710 --> 00:08:26,970
can diffuse

129
00:08:27,250 --> 00:08:30,850
into the medulla,
and from there

130
00:08:30,850 --> 00:08:32,770
into the cerebrospinal fluid.

131
00:08:35,010 --> 00:08:39,930
There, it joins the
bicarbonate buffer system on

132
00:08:39,930 --> 00:08:42,550
the other side. As you
can see here, it has just

133
00:08:42,550 --> 00:08:46,170
left the bicarbonate buffer
system on the capillary

134
00:08:46,170 --> 00:08:50,930
end, and it rejoins the
bicarbonate buffer system

135
00:08:50,930 --> 00:08:54,710
on the cerebrospinal
fluid end. And if there's

136
00:08:54,710 --> 00:08:57,870
a lot of CO2, then that
pushes the conversion

137
00:08:57,870 --> 00:09:04,490
towards carbonic acid and
lastly, bicarbonate. And

138
00:09:04,490 --> 00:09:08,870
when bicarbonate forms,
then a hydrogen ion is

139
00:09:08,870 --> 00:09:12,610
dissociated, and that is the
hydrogen that's sensed by

140
00:09:12,610 --> 00:09:16,770
the central chemoreceptors.
So, in other words,

141
00:09:17,030 --> 00:09:20,670
a buildup of carbon
dioxide, for instance,

142
00:09:20,670 --> 00:09:25,610
through increased activity,
will result in the

143
00:09:25,610 --> 00:09:29,110
formation or in the
dissociation of carbonic

144
00:09:29,110 --> 00:09:32,890
acid and the formation
of hydrogen ions, which

145
00:09:32,890 --> 00:09:35,280
can be sensed by the
central chemoreceptors.

146
00:09:35,900 --> 00:09:40,790
Upon sensing a lower pH,
which is what this is,

147
00:09:40,990 --> 00:09:44,450
the central chemoreceptors
will release serotonin,

148
00:09:44,450 --> 00:09:48,490
that's a neurotransmitter
that these neurons use,

149
00:09:48,850 --> 00:09:54,870
which will travel to the
inspiratory center, the DRG,

150
00:09:55,010 --> 00:09:58,690
which will then elicit
hyperventilation in

151
00:09:58,690 --> 00:10:02,650
response to high CO2
levels. and hyperventilation

152
00:10:02,650 --> 00:10:05,530
is defined as the
increase of breathing

153
00:10:05,530 --> 00:10:09,670
frequency and volume. So
this makes total sense.

154
00:10:09,670 --> 00:10:12,560
We have a lot of activity,
a buildup of CO2,

155
00:10:12,850 --> 00:10:16,270
the central chemoreceptors
sense a lower pH,

156
00:10:16,550 --> 00:10:20,570
and then sending
information via serotonin

157
00:10:20,570 --> 00:10:25,090
to the DRG, which
then increases the

158
00:10:25,090 --> 00:10:27,190
frequency and the
volume of breathing.

159
00:10:27,990 --> 00:10:29,810
Just as a remark,

160
00:10:29,970 --> 00:10:34,170
the expiratory center in
the ventral respiratory

161
00:10:34,170 --> 00:10:38,430
group uses GABA as
the neurotransmitter.

162
00:10:41,990 --> 00:10:43,990
So we already talked about

163
00:10:43,990 --> 00:10:45,890
the peripheral chemoreceptors.

164
00:10:46,350 --> 00:10:53,650
They are sensitive to three
chemicals or gases, to

165
00:10:53,650 --> 00:10:57,310
oxygen of course, carbon
dioxide, and also hydrogen

166
00:10:57,310 --> 00:11:00,990
ions. And as I mentioned
before, they can be either

167
00:11:00,990 --> 00:11:05,910
located in the carotid
bodies or they can be found

168
00:11:05,910 --> 00:11:10,650
in the aortic arch, in the
so-called aortic bodies.

169
00:11:11,670 --> 00:11:13,290
Depending on where they are.

170
00:11:14,490 --> 00:11:17,270
There are different
cranial nerves that pick up

171
00:11:17,270 --> 00:11:22,010
this information and sent
this to the brainstem. The

172
00:11:22,010 --> 00:11:24,090
information that comes
from the aortic bodies

173
00:11:24,090 --> 00:11:26,670
travels through the vagus
nerve, that's cranial nerve

174
00:11:26,670 --> 00:11:31,730
10 of course, and the
sensor information that

175
00:11:31,730 --> 00:11:35,010
originates from the
carotid bodies travels via

176
00:11:35,010 --> 00:11:38,830
cranial nerve 9 which is
the glossopharyngeal nerve.

177
00:11:40,650 --> 00:11:43,330
So what do they really sense

178
00:11:43,370 --> 00:11:45,770
and how about
their sensitivity?

179
00:11:46,310 --> 00:11:51,530
So the oxygen by these
peripheral chemoreceptors

180
00:11:52,990 --> 00:11:56,730
has to be very low to
be sensed by them. So

181
00:11:56,730 --> 00:11:59,550
actually the partial
pressure of oxygen has

182
00:11:59,550 --> 00:12:03,650
to be equal or less to
60 millimeters mercury

183
00:12:03,650 --> 00:12:06,970
in order to be sensed
and elicit a response.

184
00:12:07,890 --> 00:12:13,670
The CO2 sensors, they're
actually less dominant

185
00:12:13,670 --> 00:12:17,470
in the periphery because
we already have a very

186
00:12:17,470 --> 00:12:21,670
dominant system that
senses CO2 indirectly that

187
00:12:21,670 --> 00:12:24,890
we just went over in the
central nervous system.

188
00:12:26,750 --> 00:12:31,180
Another thing that's
noteworthy is that the pH can

189
00:12:31,190 --> 00:12:35,310
only be sensed by the
carotid bodies, because there

190
00:12:35,310 --> 00:12:39,630
are no sensors for hydrogen
ions in the aortic arch.

191
00:12:40,930 --> 00:12:43,410
Now, there are other
types of peripheral

192
00:12:43,410 --> 00:12:47,090
receptors that can alter
breathing. They're,

193
00:12:47,090 --> 00:12:49,230
for instance, the
irritant receptors.

194
00:12:49,610 --> 00:12:55,910
They are mainly located
in the trachea or at the

195
00:12:55,910 --> 00:12:58,390
bifurcation of the main
bronchi, as you can see

196
00:12:58,390 --> 00:13:02,490
here. They can react to
certain chemicals, to dust,

197
00:13:02,690 --> 00:13:07,530
to cold air, and their
role is to clear this area

198
00:13:07,530 --> 00:13:09,810
and make sure that
nothing dislodges further

199
00:13:09,810 --> 00:13:14,530
down the respiratory tree.
In order to do so, their

200
00:13:14,530 --> 00:13:17,770
excitation will make us
cough, and it will also

201
00:13:17,770 --> 00:13:21,710
cause bronchoconstriction
to squeeze stuff out.

202
00:13:23,310 --> 00:13:26,470
Then there are muscle
and joint receptors,

203
00:13:26,730 --> 00:13:29,410
and they are mainly found

204
00:13:29,410 --> 00:13:31,670
in the muscles of the chest.

205
00:13:33,030 --> 00:13:39,210
They sense muscle tension
and thereby they affect

206
00:13:39,210 --> 00:13:42,030
breathing. So it's
like a sensory feedback

207
00:13:42,080 --> 00:13:45,950
that tells our brainstem
and the respiratory

208
00:13:45,950 --> 00:13:51,770
center in there, much about
the state of breathing

209
00:13:51,770 --> 00:13:55,870
based on muscle function
or muscle tension.

210
00:13:58,750 --> 00:14:02,770
There are stretch receptors
and they are mainly

211
00:14:02,770 --> 00:14:09,570
found in the larger bronchi
and as you can see here,

212
00:14:10,080 --> 00:14:16,890
and bronchioles, and they
sense lung inflation.

213
00:14:17,410 --> 00:14:22,070
And the effect of
their sensation is to

214
00:14:22,830 --> 00:14:28,110
stop further inflammation
of the lungs,

215
00:14:28,110 --> 00:14:31,110
so they terminate
inflation of the lungs.

216
00:14:31,410 --> 00:14:34,870
Now, this function is part
of a reflex that's been

217
00:14:34,870 --> 00:14:38,810
very well studied and is
known as the herring borough

218
00:14:38,810 --> 00:14:43,150
inflation reflex. So
that reflex that utilizes

219
00:14:43,150 --> 00:14:47,530
these stretch receptors
basically inhibits overinflation

220
00:14:47,530 --> 00:14:50,530
of our lungs because
the tissues are very

221
00:14:50,530 --> 00:14:54,010
fragile and overinflation
would cause a lot of damage.

222
00:14:56,770 --> 00:15:00,950
Then lastly, there are
so-called J receptors.

223
00:15:01,770 --> 00:15:06,630
They are further down the
respiratory tree at the site

224
00:15:06,630 --> 00:15:11,950
of the alveoli and ducts
leading into the alveoli.

225
00:15:12,070 --> 00:15:15,990
J stands for
juxtacapillary receptors.

226
00:15:15,990 --> 00:15:19,130
They can sense
chemicals, stretch,

227
00:15:19,190 --> 00:15:22,490
and also they react
pulmonary edema.

228
00:15:22,680 --> 00:15:27,470
And once they get
activated, they affect us to

229
00:15:27,470 --> 00:15:30,810
shallow breathing to
prevent damage to those

230
00:15:30,810 --> 00:15:33,990
structures. They will lead
to bronchoconstriction

231
00:15:33,990 --> 00:15:36,900
for the same reason,
and also mucous

232
00:15:36,900 --> 00:15:40,540
secretion. So these are
all defensive activities

233
00:15:40,670 --> 00:15:45,750
that the sensation of
these receptors elicit.

234
00:15:46,050 --> 00:15:48,930
It's believed, it's
not been conclusively

235
00:15:48,930 --> 00:15:52,550
shown yet, but that
activation of the J receptors

236
00:15:52,550 --> 00:15:55,370
is actually the main
reason for dyspnea

237
00:15:55,370 --> 00:15:58,350
in patients with left
-sided heart failure.

238
00:16:02,390 --> 00:16:06,050
Here are some breathing
patterns that I

239
00:16:06,050 --> 00:16:08,890
would like you to
familiarize yourselves with.

240
00:16:08,890 --> 00:16:11,730
I'm not going to read
this whole table,

241
00:16:11,990 --> 00:16:15,000
but there are a few things

242
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:17,810
that I would like to point out.

243
00:16:17,930 --> 00:16:23,390
So there's hypnea which is
indicates normal breathing

244
00:16:23,390 --> 00:16:27,030
and normal breathing
pattern to you have already

245
00:16:27,030 --> 00:16:31,470
heard about text tachypnea
which is indicates

246
00:16:31,470 --> 00:16:36,190
increased respiratory rate
bradypnea which indicates

